<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Lesson Summary</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01470</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Lesson Summary</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b1f61680-40cd-419d-ada8-cd3d96eb61fe</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">In this lesson, you learned how to:</para>
<list id="list-00001">
  <item>Describe the effect of changing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> on a graph that represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
  <item>Use equations and graphs to compare exponential functions.</item>
</list>
<para id="para-00002">Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that helped you reach those goals:</para>
<list id="list-00002">
  <item>5.12.1: Modeling Exponential Decay <list id="list-00003">
    <item>In this activity, you practiced modeling a situation characterized by exponential decay with an equation.</item>
  </list></item>
 
  <item>5.12.2: Equations and Their Graphs  <list id="list-00004">
    <item>In this activity, you examined how the numbers <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> influence the graph representing a function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> defined by an equation of the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
    <item>5.12.2: Self Check</item>
    <item>5.12.2: Additional Resources</item>
  </list></item>

  <item>5.12.3: Graphs Representing Exponential Decay  <list id="list-00005">
    <item>In this activity, you explored how the parameter <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> influences the graph of an exponential function, which prepared you to make sense of and model situations involving repeated percentage.</item>
    <item>5.12.3: Self Check</item>
    <item>5.12.3: Additional Resources</item>
  </list></item>

  <item>5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph  <list id="list-00006">
    <item>In this activity, you determined the graphed equation that defined a given function.</item>
  </list></item>

</list>
<para id="para-00003">After these activities, you completed the following <emphasis>practice</emphasis>:</para>
<list id="list-00007">
  <item>5.12.5: Practice</item>
</list>
<note class="checking-in" id="sect-00002"><title>Checking In</title>
  <exercise id="_468785bf-5057-484e-b727-8e9294d2a234" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00004">On a scale of 1 to 5, how confident do you feel about the learning goals of this lesson?</para></problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00005">Nice reflection! You learn more when you take the time to reflect on your thinking.</para></solution></exercise></note></section>
</content>
